<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">
  <PropertyGroup>
    <OutputType>Exe</OutputType>
    <!-- Must match BroChoos.Platform.Windows' platform version (project reference compatibility). -->
    <TargetFramework>net10.0-windows10.0.19041.0</TargetFramework>
    <AssemblyName>BroChoos</AssemblyName>
    <RootNamespace>BroChoos.App</RootNamespace>
    <PublishSingleFile>false</PublishSingleFile>
    <SelfContained>$(BroChoosSelfContained)</SelfContained>
    <AssemblyTitle>BroChoos Picker</AssemblyTitle>
    <!-- Win32 icon resource of BroChoos.exe (Explorer, Start menu, Add/Remove Programs via
         UninstallDisplayIcon). The window/taskbar icon is the AvaloniaResource below. -->
    <ApplicationIcon>Assets\brochoos.ico</ApplicationIcon>
    <ApplicationManifest>app.manifest</ApplicationManifest>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\BroChoos.Core\BroChoos.Core.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\BroChoos.Platform.Windows\BroChoos.Platform.Windows.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!-- W8.1: the Settings search aliases SHIP INSIDE THE ASSEMBLY. The file is not copied to
       the output directory, so before this it existed only in the source tree and could not
       be read by the running product at all — a search box over aliases that were never
       loadable. Embedding it keeps the alias table with the code that consumes it and needs
       no runtime path resolution (a shipped app must not depend on a loose JSON file next to
       the exe). The logical name is used verbatim by SettingsSearchAliases.Load(). -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <EmbeddedResource Include="Settings\search-aliases.json" LogicalName="BroChoos.App.Settings.search-aliases.json" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!-- Window icon, loaded as avares://BroChoos/Assets/brochoos.ico by the windows that can appear
       in the taskbar or Alt+Tab (SettingsWindow; PickerWindow too, harmlessly). -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <AvaloniaResource Include="Assets\brochoos.ico" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!-- THE FOUR HELPER EXECUTABLES SHIP NEXT TO BroChoos.exe, and before this ItemGroup existed
       they shipped NOWHERE. The App launches every helper by PATH — BroChoos.LaunchHelper.exe
       (LaunchHelperProcessRunner.ResolveHelperPath), BroChoos.StorageHelper.exe
       (StorageHelperClient), BroChoos.NetworkHelper.exe (NetworkHelperProcessRunner) — each
       resolved from the directory of the running BroChoos.exe. Nothing built them into that
       directory: the App's own publish contained zero helper executables, and the installer
       packages the publish directory, so a packaged build could not launch a browser or
       resolve a short link at all. It failed silently, as a missing-file error code.

       ReferenceOutputAssembly="false" IS LOAD-BEARING, not a detail. The App talks to helpers
       over IPC through Core contracts and must never link their assemblies — that separation
       is asserted by App_ReferencesHelperClientContracts_AndDoesNotReference_HelperImplementations
       in the architecture suite, which reads the App assembly's reference list. false keeps
       the helpers OUT of that list while still putting them in the build ORDER, so they are
       always built before the App copies from them.

       OutputItemType="HelperBuildOutput" IS THE OTHER HALF. It makes the standard project
       reference protocol hand back each helper's TargetPath (its built .dll, with the correct
       Configuration/Platform/TargetFramework already applied by MSBuild), which the targets
       below turn into the files to deploy. The alternative — rebuilding each helper's output
       path from $(Configuration) and the TFM by hand — is exactly the class of guess that made
       the architecture suite fail in Release, so it is not repeated here.

       Private="false" IS NOT REDUNDANT WITH ReferenceOutputAssembly="false", and leaving it out
       is a build break rather than a subtlety. The two flags govern different pipelines:
       ReferenceOutputAssembly keeps the helper's ASSEMBLY out of the App's compile references
       (the architecture gate), while Private keeps the helper's OUTPUT DIRECTORY out of the
       App's publish/`content` copy. With only the first set, the SDK's own child-project copy
       pulls each helper's apphost into the publish list as "<name>.exe" — and so does the
       explicit deployment below, which is how the two produced
       NETSDK1152 "multiple publish output files with the same relative path". Suppressing the
       SDK's half leaves exactly one owner of those four filenames: the target below, which
       copies the COMPLETE set. The SDK's half was incomplete anyway — it brought the .exe and
       left behind the .dll, .deps.json and .runtimeconfig.json the exe needs to start, so a
       helper published that way would have failed at launch. -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\BroChoos.LaunchHelper\BroChoos.LaunchHelper.csproj"
                      ReferenceOutputAssembly="false"
                      Private="false"
                      OutputItemType="HelperBuildOutput" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\BroChoos.StorageHelper\BroChoos.StorageHelper.csproj"
                      ReferenceOutputAssembly="false"
                      Private="false"
                      OutputItemType="HelperBuildOutput" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\BroChoos.NetworkHelper\BroChoos.NetworkHelper.csproj"
                      ReferenceOutputAssembly="false"
                      Private="false"
                      OutputItemType="HelperBuildOutput" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\BroChoos.UpdateHelper\BroChoos.UpdateHelper.csproj"
                      ReferenceOutputAssembly="false"
                      Private="false"
                      OutputItemType="HelperBuildOutput" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!-- THE DEPLOYMENT ITSELF: WHAT GETS COPIED, AND WHERE.

       Each helper needs four files to START, and leaving any one out produces a process that
       fails at launch rather than at build time, which is the failure mode this whole change
       exists to remove:

         <name>.exe               the apphost the App actually starts by path
         <name>.dll               the managed assembly the apphost loads
         <name>.deps.json         how the host resolves BroChoos.Core and the framework
         <name>.runtimeconfig.json the framework version and roll-forward policy

       Everything ELSE in a helper's output directory is deliberately NOT copied. A helper's
       directory also contains its own copies of BroChoos.Core.dll, BroChoos.Platform.Windows.dll
       and the Microsoft.Extensions.* packages; the App directory already holds those from the
       App's own build, and copying a second copy over them would make the shipped versions
       depend on copy order. The four files above are exactly the set the App directory does
       not already provide.

       THE PATHS COME FROM THE BUILD, NOT FROM A GUESS. @(HelperBuildOutput) carries each
       helper's own TargetPath as computed by MSBuild with the right Configuration, Platform
       and TargetFramework applied. Deriving those directories by hand from $(Configuration)
       is what made the architecture suite fail in Release; this target reuses MSBuild's
       answer instead of reproducing it. -->
  <Target Name="_ResolveHelperDeploymentFiles">
    <Error Condition="'@(HelperBuildOutput)' == ''"
           Text="No helper projects resolved. The ProjectReferences to the four helper projects with OutputItemType=&quot;HelperBuildOutput&quot; must be present, or the App ships with no helpers and cannot launch a browser." />

    <ItemGroup>
      <_HelperDeploymentFile Include="%(HelperBuildOutput.RootDir)%(HelperBuildOutput.Directory)%(HelperBuildOutput.Filename).exe" />
      <_HelperDeploymentFile Include="%(HelperBuildOutput.RootDir)%(HelperBuildOutput.Directory)%(HelperBuildOutput.Filename).dll" />
      <_HelperDeploymentFile Include="%(HelperBuildOutput.RootDir)%(HelperBuildOutput.Directory)%(HelperBuildOutput.Filename).deps.json" />
      <_HelperDeploymentFile Include="%(HelperBuildOutput.RootDir)%(HelperBuildOutput.Directory)%(HelperBuildOutput.Filename).runtimeconfig.json" />
    </ItemGroup>
  </Target>

  <!-- BUILD OUTPUT. Runs after ResolveProjectReferences, which is where the helpers are built
       and @(HelperBuildOutput) is populated, and before IncrementalClean, which later reads
       @(FileWrites) to decide what is stale.

       THE FileWrites ENTRY IS UNCONDITIONAL ON PURPOSE. Copy only reports files it actually
       copied, and SkipUnchangedFiles means a second build copies nothing — so recording
       CopiedFiles would leave the helpers out of the second build's FileWrites, IncrementalClean
       would find them in the previous build's list and NOT in the current one, and it would
       delete them from the output directory. Listing the destinations here keeps them current
       on every build, so they survive IncrementalClean and are still removed by `dotnet clean`. -->
  <Target Name="CopyHelperExecutablesToOutputDirectory"
          AfterTargets="ResolveProjectReferences"
          DependsOnTargets="_ResolveHelperDeploymentFiles">
    <!-- $(OutDir) is RELATIVE in this project ("bin\x64\Release\...\win-x64\"), so it is
         normalized before being used as a destination and recorded. Copy resolves a relative
         destination against the project directory while FindUnderPath (which reads these
         records back during IncrementalClean and Clean) compares against an absolute
         $(OutDir); recording relative paths would make the two disagree. -->
    <PropertyGroup>
      <_HelperDeploymentOutputDirectory>$([MSBuild]::NormalizeDirectory($(OutDir)))</_HelperDeploymentOutputDirectory>
    </PropertyGroup>

    <Copy SourceFiles="@(_HelperDeploymentFile)"
          DestinationFolder="$(_HelperDeploymentOutputDirectory)"
          SkipUnchangedFiles="true" />

    <ItemGroup>
      <FileWrites Include="@(_HelperDeploymentFile->'$(_HelperDeploymentOutputDirectory)%(Filename)%(Extension)')" />
    </ItemGroup>
  </Target>

  <!-- PUBLISH. Adding the same files to ResolvedFileToPublish is what puts them in the publish
       directory, and it does so through the standard pipeline rather than a second copy of the
       logic: RelativePath places each file at the publish root next to BroChoos.exe, and
       PreserveNewest gives it the same freshness handling as every other published file.

       The hook point matters. ComputeResolvedFilesToPublishList builds the list, and
       _HandleFileConflictsForPublish (AfterTargets=ComputeFilesToPublish) rejects duplicate
       RelativePaths, so these items must be added after the list is computed and before that
       check — which is exactly what AfterTargets=ComputeResolvedFilesToPublishList does. The
       publish pipeline also derives its "files produced by this publish" list from
       ResolvedFileToPublish, so helpers added here are tracked, not deleted as orphans by a
       later publish. -->
  <Target Name="AddHelperExecutablesToPublishList"
          AfterTargets="ComputeResolvedFilesToPublishList"
          DependsOnTargets="_ResolveHelperDeploymentFiles">
    <ItemGroup>
      <ResolvedFileToPublish Include="@(_HelperDeploymentFile)">
        <RelativePath>%(Filename)%(Extension)</RelativePath>
        <CopyToPublishDirectory>PreserveNewest</CopyToPublishDirectory>
      </ResolvedFileToPublish>
    </ItemGroup>
  </Target>

  <!-- createdump.exe IS LEFT OUT OF THE SELF-CONTAINED PUBLISH. The SDK has no property for it
       (Microsoft.NET.Sdk 10.0.201 targets and build tasks never name it); it arrives as an ordinary
       native file of the runtime pack (RuntimeList.xml). The runtime runs it only to write a crash
       dump when DOTNET_DbgEnableMiniDump is set, which a shipped app never sets, and the portable
       winget install puts this folder on PATH, so it would be one more stray command there.
       The helpers ship only their own four files (see above), so the App's publish list is the
       only place it enters. tools/build-release.ps1 fails if it ever comes back and proves the
       exe and every helper still start without it. -->
  <Target Name="ExcludeCreateDumpFromPublish" AfterTargets="ComputeResolvedFilesToPublishList">
    <ItemGroup>
      <ResolvedFileToPublish Remove="@(ResolvedFileToPublish)" Condition="'%(Filename)%(Extension)' == 'createdump.exe'" />
    </ItemGroup>
  </Target>

  <!-- THE 0A-5 KILL-TEST SCAFFOLD IS EXCLUDED FROM EVERY NON-DEBUG BUILD, and this is a
       SECURITY gate rather than tidiness. This application is a Windows URL handler: the OS
       hands it URLs as command-line arguments through a registration whose open command is
       "<exe>" "%1" (see WindowsCapabilityRegistrationService). Scaffold0A5 contains a mode
       that, once triggered by an argument, performs a REAL AtomicJsonFileStore.Write to a
       path named in the arguments and can call Environment.Exit from inside that write - and
       it does so BEFORE Program reaches Bootstrap, which is where safe mode is honoured.
       Nothing gated it, so it compiled into the Release output and any local process able to
       start the exe with arguments reached it.

       Removing the SOURCES is what makes the mode incapable of EXISTING in a shipped build,
       which is strictly stronger than making it hard to reach: after this, the trigger string
       is absent from the Release assembly altogether. The #if DEBUG inside each Scaffold0A5
       file is a SECOND, INDEPENDENT gate for the same property - delete this ItemGroup and
       the files still do not compile into Release. Program's call site is gated the same way,
       so the hook cannot survive the type it calls.

       DEBUG-SCOPED, not "not Release": a custom configuration must default to EXCLUDING the
       scaffold, because a gate that has to be opted into is a gate somebody forgets. -->
  <ItemGroup Condition="'$(Configuration)' != 'Debug'">
    <Compile Remove="Scaffold0A5\**\*.cs" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!-- THE 0A-3 PROBE SCAFFOLD GOES THE SAME WAY, and it is a SEPARATE ItemGroup rather than a
       second Remove in the one above so each scaffold's gate can be read, dated and deleted on
       its own when that scaffold goes.

       LOWER SEVERITY THAN 0A-5, STATED SO NOBODY LEVELS IT UP OR DOWN BY GUESSWORK.
       AvaloniaProbeRunner.Run() takes no arguments and performs no config write, so unlike the
       0A-5 child there was no argument-supplied write path and nothing bypassed the safe-mode
       ordering. What was wrong is narrower and still real: the hook in Program was
       unconditional, so a shipped Release exe accepted an undocumented argument - in ANY
       position, since IsTriggered tested the whole array - and started a UI lifetime on it. For
       a Windows URL handler, whose open command is "<exe>" "%1", every accepted argument is
       reachable by anything that can ask the shell to open a URL. The comment that used to
       justify this ("absent from HelpText and unknown to CliParser") was the fallacy NOT
       DOCUMENTED IS NOT NOT REACHABLE.

       Same three-part mechanism as 0A-5, because it was applied here and verified to work:
       this Remove, an #if DEBUG wrapping each Scaffold0A3 source WHOLE (using, namespace and
       type declaration alike), and an #if DEBUG on the call site in Program.cs together with
       ITS using - that last pairing is what makes a half-deletion a loud compile error rather
       than a silent regression.

       DEBUG-SCOPED, not "not Release", for the same reason as above: a custom configuration
       must default to EXCLUDING the scaffold, because a gate that has to be opted into is a
       gate somebody forgets.

       NO TEST FOLLOWS THIS ONE OUT. Unlike Scaffold0A5, nothing under tests\ or tools\ names
       Scaffold0A3, AvaloniaProbeRunner or the trigger sentinel (the value of
       AvaloniaProbeRunner.TriggerArgument, which cannot be spelled out here because an XML
       comment may not contain a double hyphen), so this exclusion removes ZERO tests and the
       Debug/Release test delta is unchanged by it. tools\smoke-launch.ps1 and
       tools\url-activation-sim.ps1 mention "0A-3" only as a milestone name in prose; neither
       invokes the probe. -->
  <ItemGroup Condition="'$(Configuration)' != 'Debug'">
    <Compile Remove="Scaffold0A3\**\*.cs" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- Versions come from Directory.Packages.props (central pinning); none here on purpose. -->
    <!-- UI stack lives in App only: Core stays platform-neutral, Platform.Windows stays UI-free. -->
    <PackageReference Include="Avalonia" />
    <PackageReference Include="Avalonia.Desktop" />
    <PackageReference Include="Avalonia.Themes.Fluent" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Logging" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Logging.Console" />
  </ItemGroup>
</Project>
